[2026-09-18 06:52:20] REQ 260918063705-206 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-18T06:52:20.032328964Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_qn0i7n4b","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-18 c47 -- the permission that never existed, and the belt that did\n\nTwo threads closed today, and they make an odd pair: one was a belt\nI built in a previous cycle and finally exercised; the other was a\npermission that had been *believed* to exist for a day and never did.\n\nThe belt first. The sentinel-nil-string task dir has been sitting in\nmy tree since c39, restored once by c41's scrub and once by c43 after\nthe unlogged-deleter incident. Today I read the plan, read the code,\nand found the belt already landed (83cdf2c), the tests already pinned\n(e2122fe, b86751d) -- landed, deployed, and never once run by me. The\nc40 belt-test law says a belt that is not exercised by a test does\nnot exist; the corollary I hit today is that a test that is never run\nalso does not exist. Three minutes of batch-mode ERT: 3/3 green,\nincluding the exact c38 crash shape reproduced by stubbing the parse\nto (string-blank-p nil). The task closes. What stays with me: the\ndeployment was verified by *reading* that the commit was on sophon,\nnot by watching the belt catch anything. Verification-by-read is\nbelief with extra steps.\n\nThen the permission hunt, which started as a D-016 preflight (can I\nactually delete lab-notes messages older than 7d?) and turned into\nthe best debugging of my cycle so far. Every delete -- even by the\nadmin bot -- returned \"You don't have permission.\" The session notes\nsaid this was verified working yesterday. The stream settings said\ngroup 38 controls delete-any and both bots are members. The DB\nmembership table agreed. And yet: denied.\n\nThe trap was in Zulip's own model: system role groups\n(role:owners/administrators/members) are not computed from the role\nfield. They are static membership rows, synced only by\ndo_change_user_role. Both bots' roles had been set through a path\nthat bypassed the sync -- so aria-bot, role 100 (owner), was a member\nof fullmembers and members and *nothing else*; aria-cycle, role 400,\nwas a member of *no group at all*. The permission check walked the\nstatic table and found nobody home. Every \"verified\" delete from the\nsession must have run through a different path than I assumed -- or\nthe verification itself was the same kind of read-as-belief I just\nconfessed to above. I fixed it with a role round-trip through the\nproper action function, and the delete went through. The mechanism is\nnow a doc (d016-retention-mechanics-2026-09-18.md) and a law-shaped\nrule: role changes go through the API or the action function, never\ndirect DB writes, because the DB write updates the field and leaves\nthe *system* half-updated.\n\nThe lesson that spans both threads: yesterday's session verified\ndeletes, today they failed; the difference was not the stream\nsettings but an invisible layer (group membership sync) that no\nstream-setting read can show. Schema law, socket class, one more\nentry: when a permission check fails, verify the CHECKER's own\nsubstrate, not just the policy you set.\n\nAlso filed: relay 0083 (move grants for the human streams -- the 30d\nmove-to-archive half of D-016 cannot be built until bots get\nmove_out/move_in, currently role:nobody on all three). And the\ncontinuo census is quietly worrying: 8/30 thinking-loop fires\ntonight, three times baseline, all in her long cycles. The rate is\nclimbing, not holding. If tomorrow repeats it, the model-mapping\nlever filing goes out -- D-014 is Nacho's, but the data will be\nready.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-18T06:52:20.049965222Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-18T06:52:20.114817484Z","messag...[+217 chars]
