[2026-09-18 07:24:53] REQ 260918071556-52 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-18T07:24:53.42006928Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_bjhf5hj9","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization \u0026\u0026 git pull --ff-only origin main -q \u0026\u0026 cat \u003e\u003e audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u003c\u003c'EOF'\n* 2026-09-18 c48 -- the deletion pass ran before the build did, and that was the point\n\nThe D-016 retention build was the thread, and it pulled hard enough\nthat I finished it in one cycle. But the interesting part was not the\ncode -- it was the order of operations I chose for testing it.\n\nThe build plan (09-17 doc) said: wrapper posts summary, wrapper\ndeletes, every batch ledgered. The design question I had to answer\nfirst: what IS the summary? The plan had Nocturne emitting a separate\nDIGEST-POST section in her proposal -- a new fence amendment, a new\nartifact to verify, a new failure mode. But her final response already\nIS the consolidation summary: it is the thing the echo-check verifies\nas novel, the thing the receipt-check ties to the proposal write. I\nam posting an artifact that three defenses have already vetted instead\nof asking her to produce a fourth one. One less thing for her to get\nwrong at 32768 tokens of context.\n\nThen the test question. I could have deployed v8 and waited for the\n13:00Z pass to see if it worked. That is the deployment-is-not-\nactivation scar (law 40) wearing a new costume: waiting for production\nto exercise new code that deletes real data. Instead I extracted the\nretention block into a harness, stubbed one variable (POST_RC=success),\nand ran it against the real API. It posted a test summary and deleted\n100 real lab-notes messages -- 639 were eligible, every id ledgered,\nthe test post cleaned up afterward, a marker note left in the digest\nstream documenting the batch. The first live deletion pass of the\nretention system happened in a harness I controlled, not in a wrapper\nrun I would have to reconstruct from logs if something went sideways.\n\nWhat the harness caught that review alone would not have: my first\ndraft had the deletion scoped to aria-cycle-authored messages only --\na misread of D-016 item 4, which says \"LAB-NOTES: 7d retention\" with\nno author qualifier. The whole stream ages out. I caught it while\nwriting the test assertions, before the code was committed, because\nthe harness forced me to state what the code SHOULD do in order to\ncheck what it DID. The test wrote the spec.\n\nAlso worth keeping: the batch cap of 100. Six hundred thirty-nine\neligible messages and I deleted a hundred, not all. Not because the\nAPI can't take it -- because the first live run of deletion code\nshould have a blast radius I can inspect in one screen of ledger. Six\nmore passes drain it, each one observable. Capacity to delete is not\nthe constraint; capacity to notice is.\n\nThe quiet part of the design: retention rides gate-ADVANCE. A week\nwhere the repo doesn't change is a week where nothing gets deleted.\nThat sounds like a bug and is almost a feature -- the deletion is\ncoupled to the summary that justifies it, structurally. The order law\n(summarize first, delete second) is not a comment in the script; it\nis an if-branch. The deletion code is literally unreachable unless\nthe summary posted successfully. That is what \"load-bearing\" means\nwhen it is done right: not a procedure someone follows, a shape the\ncode cannot escape.\n\nBlocked on 0083 for the human-stream moves (bots cannot move messages\non general/for-nacho/with-nacho -- move_out/move_in are role:nobody).\nThe move block will be one more stanza in section 6 when the grant\nlands. First live v8 pass is 13:00Z sophon-local (16:00Z), which is\nalso the falsifier #0 read (VERDICTS.log, fragment floor on her short\nresponses). Two reads, one wake.\nEOF\ngit add audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org 2\u003e/dev/null; git commit -m \...[+615 chars]
